WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:07.371
Soundtrack for the apocalypse is silent.

00:00:08.439 --> 00:00:11.242
We felt the weight
of that total absence of sound

00:00:11.275 --> 00:00:15.079
when we arrived in the devastated
neighborhoods of Altadena

00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:16.647
and the Palisades

00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:19.917
after the January 2025 firestorm.

00:00:20.785 --> 00:00:25.389
Our small crew of two or three
was usually alone for blocks and blocks.

00:00:25.923 --> 00:00:30.628
There was no sound of cars or children
or people or pool pumps

00:00:30.661 --> 00:00:32.763
or electricity across the lines.

00:00:33.297 --> 00:00:34.999
Even the birds were gone.

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:41.072
I'm best known for having pioneered
something called immersive journalism,

00:00:41.105 --> 00:00:43.541
which uses emerging technologies

00:00:43.541 --> 00:00:46.077
to create new stories
that represent our world

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.878
the way it really is:

00:00:47.912 --> 00:00:50.981
fully dimensional and not flat.

00:00:51.682 --> 00:00:54.952
People then can experience
stories with their whole body

00:00:54.985 --> 00:00:56.921
and not just with their eyes.

00:00:58.522 --> 00:00:59.990
Also, as a journalist,

00:01:00.024 --> 00:01:05.029
I feel that we have a moral responsibility
to bear witness to injustice,

00:01:05.062 --> 00:01:07.431
to wonder, to the human condition.

00:01:07.998 --> 00:01:09.767
With immersive journalism,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.437
I am merging those new technologies
with that code of ethics.

00:01:14.004 --> 00:01:15.840
I'm also founding director

00:01:15.873 --> 00:01:18.309
for the Narrative
and Emerging Media program

00:01:18.309 --> 00:01:20.444
at Arizona State University.

00:01:20.478 --> 00:01:22.012
So when the fires broke out,

00:01:22.046 --> 00:01:25.082
I mobilized my colleagues
and my graduate students,

00:01:25.116 --> 00:01:26.951
and we went out to cover,

00:01:26.951 --> 00:01:29.720
using our Cronkite journalism news passes

00:01:29.753 --> 00:01:33.190
what firemen called “Nuketown.”

00:01:34.125 --> 00:01:36.393
When we got there,
the fires were basically out,

00:01:36.393 --> 00:01:38.729
so there wasn't anything
anyone could really do.

00:01:38.762 --> 00:01:41.665
But when we'd run into a fire crew,
they would rush over,

00:01:41.699 --> 00:01:44.835
and we thought, "Wow, they're going
to question our press passes."

00:01:44.835 --> 00:01:47.838
But no, they just wanted to help
in any way that they could.

00:01:47.872 --> 00:01:52.009
And in our case, it was just
water or granola bars.

00:01:53.544 --> 00:01:57.314
All of us were suffering
from shock and sorrow

00:01:57.314 --> 00:02:01.752
as the sole witnesses
for the devastation around us.

00:02:03.787 --> 00:02:05.623
It was grueling work.

00:02:05.656 --> 00:02:08.325
The air was toxic, it was incredibly hot.

00:02:08.692 --> 00:02:11.362
We were wearing hazmat suits
and heavy boots

00:02:11.395 --> 00:02:13.130
and double-filtered masks.

00:02:13.731 --> 00:02:17.368
We were using specialized equipment
designed by Mike Caronna,

00:02:17.401 --> 00:02:21.238
who is a photojournalist and the former
general manager for Reuters.

00:02:21.272 --> 00:02:25.476
We had 3D-printed special parts
that ... clamped to the top,

00:02:25.509 --> 00:02:27.678
middle and bottom of a pole,

00:02:27.711 --> 00:02:30.548
so we could shoot
in every direction all at once.

00:02:31.248 --> 00:02:34.618
Each one of those dots
represents a shot that we took,

00:02:34.618 --> 00:02:38.355
and then [they] would be fused together
using artificial intelligence.

00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:42.826
I also used my phone to make
very painstaking captures.

00:02:43.194 --> 00:02:46.096
You have to use your camera
like a paintbrush

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.399
and get every bit, every angle,

00:02:48.432 --> 00:02:49.733
and at the same time,

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:52.369
make sure that you're not
stepping on upturned nails

00:02:52.403 --> 00:02:54.772
or broken glass or shards of metal.

00:02:55.773 --> 00:02:58.375
This is my graduate student,
Ashley Buschhorn.

00:02:58.776 --> 00:03:02.079
She was trying to capture a cactus
that had been really weirdly burned

00:03:02.112 --> 00:03:03.681
only on one side.

00:03:07.384 --> 00:03:09.987
I'd often look up and see
my colleague Mary Mathieson,

00:03:09.987 --> 00:03:13.724
and she’d been taking the gear into
these incredibly dangerous situations,

00:03:13.757 --> 00:03:16.427
determined to get the best scan possible.

00:03:16.427 --> 00:03:18.495
We scanned fallen angels,

00:03:21.265 --> 00:03:22.533
buddhas,

00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:27.271
melted trash cans,

00:03:29.139 --> 00:03:30.407
Teslas

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.411
[and] surf wagons.

00:03:37.581 --> 00:03:40.718
We filmed until the light
was gone, every day.

00:03:42.419 --> 00:03:44.154
We even used our headlights

00:03:44.188 --> 00:03:47.458
to capture this car
that was sliding down the mountain.

00:03:47.491 --> 00:03:49.793
We didn't know if it would
be there the next day.

00:03:50.861 --> 00:03:52.363
More than once,

00:03:52.363 --> 00:03:56.133
I'd have to leave the crew working
and go sit in the car by myself.

00:03:57.368 --> 00:04:00.070
These weren't just places on a map.

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:02.139
I grew up in and around
these neighborhoods,

00:04:02.172 --> 00:04:05.242
and often we were scanning
the homes of friends of mine.

00:04:05.276 --> 00:04:06.777
They hadn't even been back yet,

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.346
they didn't even know the status
of their own places.

00:04:10.114 --> 00:04:11.448
Like the cello teacher.

00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:14.118
This is a home we brought my son to

00:04:14.118 --> 00:04:15.819
for years, every weekend.

00:04:19.256 --> 00:04:23.093
This is a home where I held
the baby shower for my first child.

00:04:27.598 --> 00:04:30.100
This is my sister's
good friend's equipment.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.903
She taught horseback riding
at Will Rogers Park,

00:04:32.903 --> 00:04:34.305
and when the fires broke out,

00:04:34.338 --> 00:04:36.807
she rushed over to go rescue the horses,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.609
and then her own home burned,

00:04:38.642 --> 00:04:41.445
and everything she owned
was also destroyed.

00:04:41.445 --> 00:04:43.380
This was a particularly difficult moment

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.285
because I was standing next to the wall
that had collapsed over a bedroom,

00:04:48.319 --> 00:04:51.722
where I said goodbye to one
of my best friends about a year earlier.

00:04:51.755 --> 00:04:54.258
She had died of cancer the next day.

00:04:54.258 --> 00:04:56.527
Her partner escaped with a small suitcase

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.897
and a loaf of bread
from their favorite bakery.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:02.433
The thumbnail was completely black,

00:05:02.466 --> 00:05:04.001
which is incredibly strange

00:05:04.001 --> 00:05:07.838
because none of my other thumbnails
were like that on any of my scans.

00:05:07.871 --> 00:05:09.907
There was also some incredible stories.

00:05:09.907 --> 00:05:13.877
This was a house where my son
spent most weekends during high school

00:05:13.911 --> 00:05:15.546
with a bunch of his buddies.

00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:18.449
One of his buddies had been there
the night before the fire,

00:05:18.449 --> 00:05:21.785
and he left his car in front
of the house and took an Uber home

00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:23.420
because they'd been out partying.

00:05:23.454 --> 00:05:25.556
They saw on Instagram an image

00:05:25.556 --> 00:05:28.659
which seemed to indicate
his car was totally intact.

00:05:29.126 --> 00:05:32.363
So I called him when we got there
to describe what we were seeing,

00:05:32.396 --> 00:05:34.031
and it was extraordinary.

00:05:34.064 --> 00:05:36.300
Other than ashes on top,
his car looked [to be]

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.535
in perfect condition.

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:41.438
He later got into the car
and just drove it away.

00:05:42.106 --> 00:05:44.541
Then there was the Bank
of America building,

00:05:44.575 --> 00:05:47.177
where a lot of people
had their safe deposit boxes,

00:05:47.211 --> 00:05:49.179
and they were told they'd been destroyed.

00:05:49.179 --> 00:05:52.516
They'd lost family heirlooms
and Holocaust papers.

00:05:52.916 --> 00:05:57.421
But it turns out, underneath
all this crazy rubble --

00:05:57.454 --> 00:05:59.123
everything was safe.

00:05:59.123 --> 00:06:01.725
And here we were, taking these images,

00:06:01.759 --> 00:06:03.794
and there was nobody there guarding it.

00:06:05.963 --> 00:06:08.966
Niantic, the company
that makes Pokémon Go,

00:06:08.966 --> 00:06:10.934
learned about the work we were doing,

00:06:10.968 --> 00:06:13.170
and they used our material

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:16.707
to try to help folks
who'd been impacted by the fire.

00:06:17.074 --> 00:06:19.376
And as a journalist,
that's what you want, right?

00:06:19.376 --> 00:06:20.811
You want your work to be used

00:06:20.811 --> 00:06:24.014
in a way that can make an impact,
that can help others.

00:06:25.115 --> 00:06:26.717
I also had a third motivation

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.386
about why I was doing
the work I was doing.

00:06:30.854 --> 00:06:34.057
I really understood
personally the helplessness

00:06:34.057 --> 00:06:37.995
and just the feelings of loss,

00:06:38.028 --> 00:06:40.531
because a year and a half earlier,

00:06:40.531 --> 00:06:42.132
I'd had my own fire.

00:06:51.975 --> 00:06:55.913
This was my home studio
where I'd lost everything personally

00:06:55.913 --> 00:06:58.515
and professionally from
pretty much my entire life.

00:06:58.549 --> 00:07:02.019
During COVID, I had closed up
my offices in Santa Monica

00:07:02.052 --> 00:07:03.954
and I'd brought everything here.

00:07:03.987 --> 00:07:05.622
I mean, no one was coming to work,

00:07:05.656 --> 00:07:08.792
so this is where I thought
it would be safe.

00:07:11.995 --> 00:07:13.197
It turned out,

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.266
my electric bicycle battery had exploded.

00:07:17.935 --> 00:07:20.871
I lost projects and equipment.

00:07:21.305 --> 00:07:24.975
I lost photos and video archives,

00:07:24.975 --> 00:07:28.245
diaries, family memories.

00:07:29.413 --> 00:07:30.714
I inhaled smoke.

00:07:30.714 --> 00:07:32.015
I burned my gums.

00:07:32.049 --> 00:07:33.617
I damaged my lungs.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.020
And I now have what might be
a permanent neuropathy

00:07:37.054 --> 00:07:38.355
in my esophagus.

00:07:40.057 --> 00:07:45.329
When I looked at the remains
of what had been decades of my life,

00:07:45.362 --> 00:07:47.130
I just thought it was all gone.

00:07:48.765 --> 00:07:52.369
But a few strange things
actually survived.

00:07:52.402 --> 00:07:55.939
This is a photo I took in college
of one of my best friends.

00:07:56.707 --> 00:07:59.142
My husband of 27 years ago,

00:07:59.142 --> 00:08:03.380
he wrote me this love letter
after our first date and it said,

00:08:03.413 --> 00:08:07.017
"I think I see a kindred spirit in you,
and if you invite me,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.353
I'll cross the country in a heartbeat."

00:08:09.386 --> 00:08:13.056
Well, I invited him and he asked me
to marry him on our second date.

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:16.894
I also used my scans with my battle
with the insurance company.

00:08:16.894 --> 00:08:18.929
Like, they didn't want to pay on this sofa

00:08:18.962 --> 00:08:20.497
because I didn’t have a receipt,

00:08:20.497 --> 00:08:22.566
and the receipt
had been burned in the fire.

00:08:22.566 --> 00:08:25.168
My scans showed very clearly
there was a sofa in there,

00:08:25.168 --> 00:08:26.537
and they had to pay on that.

00:08:26.537 --> 00:08:28.872
And it was one of the reasons
why I was out there,

00:08:28.906 --> 00:08:30.207
scanning everybody's homes.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.311
I wanted them to be able to use
what we had in any way that they could.

00:08:34.745 --> 00:08:37.981
Another thing strangely survived the fire.

00:08:38.015 --> 00:08:40.684
In 2009, I was at an event

00:08:40.684 --> 00:08:43.153
at the University of Southern California,

00:08:43.186 --> 00:08:45.689
and Mark Hamill was at the adjacent table.

00:08:46.089 --> 00:08:49.159
Now, I grew up in LA,
and normally I don't bug celebrities.

00:08:49.192 --> 00:08:52.296
I just let them go
about their lives peacefully, right?

00:08:52.329 --> 00:08:55.098
But my son was six and I couldn't resist.

00:08:55.132 --> 00:08:57.768
I asked Mark Hamill to sign
the program card.

00:08:58.735 --> 00:09:00.504
And that flammable,

00:09:00.537 --> 00:09:02.873
fragile piece of paper,

00:09:02.906 --> 00:09:05.475
it somehow survived a lithium-battery fire

00:09:05.509 --> 00:09:07.210
that was so hot

00:09:07.244 --> 00:09:10.047
that the fire folks said
it had to burn its course.

00:09:10.414 --> 00:09:15.018
And there it was, like, it drifted
right on top of the hellscape

00:09:15.052 --> 00:09:17.120
that used to be my entire life.

00:09:18.488 --> 00:09:21.391
And not only that,
Mark Hamill wrote a message.

00:09:23.193 --> 00:09:26.129
"The force will be with you always.

00:09:26.163 --> 00:09:27.898
Your pal, Mark Hamill."

00:09:28.532 --> 00:09:31.401
I can't help but take
that message literally.

00:09:32.169 --> 00:09:35.172
The force, it's within us all.

00:09:35.739 --> 00:09:38.442
And it's not what we lose that defines us.

00:09:38.875 --> 00:09:40.277
It's who we love

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:42.980
and how we take that love forward.

00:09:44.047 --> 00:09:45.582
I know personally,

00:09:45.616 --> 00:09:48.151
when you’re looking at
what [are] the remains

00:09:48.185 --> 00:09:50.454
of what had been a vibrant life,

00:09:50.487 --> 00:09:53.123
you can't help but feel
like everything is gone.

00:09:54.224 --> 00:09:56.793
But silence is not emptiness.

00:09:57.127 --> 00:10:00.263
It's a place to reflect and remember,

00:10:00.297 --> 00:10:03.867
to imagine the  wild ways we can rebuild

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:06.903
and to consider what really endures.

00:10:07.571 --> 00:10:11.808
And now, as we come to the one-year
anniversary of the fires,

00:10:11.808 --> 00:10:15.379
I'm calling on everyone to feel the force,

00:10:15.412 --> 00:10:17.814
to think about that love within you

00:10:17.848 --> 00:10:20.083
and help support our friends,

00:10:20.117 --> 00:10:22.319
our neighbors, our communities.

00:10:22.986 --> 00:10:26.223
Together, we can rebuild Los Angeles,

00:10:26.256 --> 00:10:28.458
better and more resilient.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:30.827
Thank you.

00:10:30.861 --> 00:10:33.330
(Applause)

